\title{variplex: analysis of strong dependency and conflict graphs of variability models}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='variplex logo' width='120'}}
variplex computes, for a variability model, every relationship between two
features that holds in all its valid configurations, and analyses the
resulting graphs.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=vp_generate]{vp_generate()}} reads a feature model in UVL, or the Boolean formula
(DIMACS) of a model written in any other notation, such as the Kconfig
files of Linux, and computes the \strong{requires}, \strong{excludes} and
\strong{at-least-one} graphs with a SAT solver.
\item More than a hundred functions, all named \verb{vp_*}, answer questions about the
graphs and return tibbles: measures of each feature, the hierarchy,
conflicts and their causes, configuration, teams, combinatorial testing,
network patterns and the evolution of a model through its versions.
\code{\link[=vp_functions]{vp_functions()}} lists them by topic.
\item \code{\link[=vp_plot]{vp_plot()}} draws a model or part of it; \code{\link[=vp_dot]{vp_dot()}} gives the Graphviz
source of the drawing.
}

Start with \code{vignette("variplex")}. The graphs hold relationships between
\strong{two} features only: a rule that ties three or more features at once is in
no graph, unless it implies a relationship between two of them
(\code{vignette("limits-theory", package = "variplex")}).

The engine is Strong4VM (\url{https://github.com/rheradio/strong4vm}), whose C++
code, with the SAT solver Glucose 3.0, the backbone solver BoneDigger and the
ANTLR 4 parser runtime, is compiled into the package.
